{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUU91RPVnYggQMfzWxCJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Bottom line: 3PL, by a wide margin, at current volumes.**\n\n**The math**\n\nWest-coast volume: 4,500/wk × 35% = 1,575 orders/wk ≈ 82,000 orders/year.\n\n- **3PL cost:** 82,000 × $3.10 ≈ **$254,000/year**, scales linearly with volume, no lock-in.\n- **Warehouse cost:** the $420k lease is only part of the bill. Add 8 hires (fully loaded, say $45–55k each → ~$400k) plus equipment, WMS/systems integration, insurance, and packaging materials (call it another $100–150k). All-in, you're likely looking at **$900k–$1M/year**, before any setup capex, and locked in for 3 years regardless of volume.\n\n**Breakeven:** Even using just the bare $420k lease (ignoring the 8 hires entirely), you'd need ~135,000 orders/year (~2,600/wk) on the west coast to match 3PL cost — 65% above where you are today. Once you add labor and overhead, breakeven volume is closer to 5,500+ orders/week west coast alone, which exceeds your *entire current company* order volume. 3PL providers also typically get cheaper per-order with scale, which widens this gap further rather than closing it.\n\n**Why not split?**\nSplitting doesn't reduce the fundamental problem: the warehouse's costs are mostly fixed, so partial volume through it doesn't amortize the lease/headcount any better — you'd just be paying full fixed cost for the warehouse *and* variable 3PL cost on top, at a total spend higher than either option alone. Splitting only makes sense as an operational hedge (e.g., 3PL for overflow/peak) once a warehouse already exists — it's not a way to save money at this stage.\n\n**What would flip the answer**\n- West-coast volume growing roughly 2–3x from here (i.e., company-wide volume nearly doubling if the 35% share holds).\n- A hard requirement for packaging/branding control or returns handling that's costing you materially in NPS, refund fraud, or brand damage today — something the $650k+/year cost gap would need to be weighed against.\n- 3PL delivery/quality reliability actually failing to hit 2-day SLA in practice (worth a pilot before committing either way).\n\n**Recommendation:** go 3PL now. Structure the contract with a volume-based renegotiation clause or an exit point, and revisit the warehouse build when west-coast volume is tracking toward ~2.5–3x today's level or when packaging/returns control becomes a demonstrated revenue/brand problem rather than a preference. A 3-year lease is the wrong instrument for a \"growing but not yet huge\" segment — it removes your ability to react to how west-coast demand actually develops.\n\nCaveat: I estimated your per-hire loaded cost (~$50k) and overhead (~$100–150k) since those weren't given — swap in your real numbers and the breakeven volume will shift, but you'd need those numbers to come in far below typical warehouse-labor costs for the conclusion to flip."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":40626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c620c34b-2b06-4dea-a611-963ed49b17ce","uuid":"c62acf47-0783-4c19-98eb-10353fda886a","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:07:58.493Z","request_id":"req_011CfUU912qBrwhwtxYp4TYD"}
